I’ll first inspect the metadata, diffraction range, and the reusable refinement helpers, then build a chemically constrained phase inventory before refining any profile terms. I’ll keep candidate screening and phase-by-phase residual evidence explicit, and only export from a reopened, replay-checked GSAS-II project.

The chemistry points first to Mn–W–O products, but I’m treating MnWO₄, both precursors, polymorphs, and plausible off-stoichiometric oxides as hypotheses rather than assumptions. Next I’m extracting peak families and auditing the local candidate records (composition, symmetry, occupancies, source conditions, and geometry) before any Rietveld fitting.

The diagnostic reflections identify wolframite MnWO₄ decisively: the split 29.58/30.03° pair, 18.06°, 23.29°, 23.80°, 30.91°, and 35.7° family all align with one monoclinic P2/c cell. I rejected two newer FeWO₄ analogue records because they contain impossible 1.31–1.50 Å W–O contacts; the older FeWO₄ parent is ordered, ambient-condition, and geometrically sound. I’m now adapting that parent by Fe→Mn and using residual-only evidence to test WO₃/Mn oxides and Mn₃WO₆.

The first clean refinement confirms a second coherent family that MnWO₄ cannot generate: 22.70°, 26.16°, 28.2–28.5°, 32.83°, 33.74°, 41.47°, and 54–56°. That family is the monoclinic WO₃ precursor, not random residual. I’m comparing all local WO₃ records now; several are unusable because of impossible O–O contacts or high-pressure/source-condition mismatch, so I’m keeping the nuisance model fixed while testing the physically sound ambient polymorphs.

The ambient monoclinic WO₃ record is already strongly supported: adding it drops Rwp from 16.79% to 14.18% and gives a positive ~15.2 wt% fraction before profile/atomic refinement. Its unique 22.70° and 32.83° reflections are present. I’m finishing matched-condition trials of triclinic, orthorhombic, tetragonal, and hexagonal alternatives so the polymorph call does not rest on chemistry alone.

The third phase is residual tetragonal Mn₃O₄, not Mn₂O₃ or Mn₃WO₆. Its unique 17.51°, 28.50°, 31.80°, 43.85°, 50.30°, and 59.64° peaks form a consistent family; its refined cell stays close to the ambient hausmannite record. The provisional fractions also close the precursor balance (Mn:W ≈ 1.03), whereas the two-phase model is strongly W-rich. I also selected sample displacement over zero shift because it gives the better angular trend; zero is now fixed and will not be co-refined. Next I’m establishing the minimal common width baseline before testing phase-specific size/strain.

The phase inventory is now stable at wolframite MnWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ + hausmannite Mn₃O₄, with the third phase supported by both diagnostic peaks and precursor mass balance. I’m now testing the common instrumental width model and phase-specific broadening branches before the final atomic replay and exports.

The width tests favor a single positive common Lorentzian term (`X`) on the sharp-phase baseline. Adding `W` makes the pair strongly correlated, while models containing `U` drive it negative, so those branches are being pruned. I’m checking only bounded low-angle asymmetry next, then each phase’s size-only and strain-only response from neutral values.

A chemically cleaner cross-check changed the main structural parent: ambient ZnWO₄ and MgWO₄ prototypes fit the MnWO₄ intensities far better than the old FeWO₄ record under the identical nuisance model; ZnWO₄ is slightly best. I’m therefore using the ordered 293 K ZnWO₄ neutron structure with only Zn→Mn substitution, then repeating the broadening challenges from that reconverged branch so the profile decision is not inherited from the poorer parent.

The accepted profile is now low-dimensional and stable: common `X` only, plus isotropic strain for the visibly broader WO₃ and Mn₃O₄ components; neither term is strongly correlated, and size-only branches ran to bounds and were rejected. A disk-reopened atomic replay shows that refining the dominant MnWO₄ coordinates is supported; I’m screening the resulting bond geometry before deciding between cation-only and all-coordinate models.

The final full-range save/reopen replay converged reproducibly at Rwp 9.055% with 8,301 observations. The submitted crystalline fractions are 71.92% MnWO₄, 18.28% WO₃, and 9.80% Mn₃O₄; I’m doing the last consistency checks on the exported CIF geometry, CSV arrays, project flags, and residual peak inventory before writing the report.

Identified three crystalline phases:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| MnWO₄, wolframite | 71.92 wt% |
| WO₃, monoclinic | 18.28 wt% |
| Mn₃O₄, hausmannite | 9.80 wt% |

Final replay: Rwp 9.055%, Rp 6.020%, GOF 3.642. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material. Minor-phase fractions are profile-sensitive; realistic ranges are discussed in the report.

